\f0\b\fs42 \cf2 \strokec2 4. How a completed research run will work\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Question arrives:
\f1\b0  the browser sends one broad question to 
\f4\fs25\fsmilli12667 \cb11 control-plane
\f1\fs28 \cb1 .\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Planning:
\f1\b0  an AI planner turns it into roughly 6\'968 flat sub-questions. \'93Flat\'94 means none depends on another this month, so all can start immediately.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Fan-out:
\f1\b0  
\f4\fs25\fsmilli12667 \cb11 control-plane
\f1\fs28 \cb1  publishes the sub-tasks to 
\f4\fs25\fsmilli12667 \cb11 research.subtasks
\f1\fs28 \cb1  in Redpanda/Kafka.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Research:
\f1\b0  researcher workers ask 
\f4\fs25\fsmilli12667 \cb11 retrieval-service
\f1\fs28 \cb1  to search and fetch sources. They do not bypass it.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Fan-in:
\f1\b0  every completed sub-task atomically increments a Postgres counter. Exactly one worker observes \'93all expected tasks are done\'94 and triggers the writer.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	6	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Writing:
\f1\b0  the writer creates structured claims. A claim has text plus one source ID, rather than loose prose with vaguely related footnotes.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	7	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Verification:
\f1\b0  the critic re-fetches the cited source, finds the best evidence passage, and marks a claim supported, partial, unsupported, contradicted, or unreachable.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	8	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Presentation:
\f1\b0  the browser receives live progress through SSE and eventually sees a report with visible verification badges.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32\fsmilli16380 \cf8 \strokec8 The fan-in decision: why Postgres instead of a Java counter?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs28 \cf5 \strokec5 An in-memory 
\f4\fs25\fsmilli12667 \cb11 AtomicInteger
\f1\fs28 \cb1  is like a tally counter kept in one person\'92s pocket. If that person restarts, it is gone. If two copies of the service run, each copy has a different tally. Instead, Postgres owns the tally:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs23\fsmilli11733 \cf12 \cb13 \strokec12 UPDATE dag_levels\
SET completed = completed + 1\
WHERE run_id = ? AND level = 0\
RETURNING completed, expected;\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs28 \cf5 \cb1 \strokec5 The database makes the increment atomic. One and only one completion sees the final count. Because it is in Postgres, the count survives an application restart.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa348\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs42 \cf2 \strokec2 5. What is deliberately not being built\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

